\f1\fs22 Tony ran from the scene of the crime\'97for surely he\'92d caused that poor drunk\'92s death, hadn\'92t he? As far as he knew, getting people sliced in half by a capriciously solidifying wall would not be received well among polite society, although polite society typically cared little for the likes of such a drunk. If anything, the man\'92s family might be sued for desecrating Airborne property with inferior blood and guts. Nevertheless, Tony was taking no chances. He fled inward, leaving a trail of bloody and grisly organic shoe prints on the polished floor\'97except for the spot where he\'92d tripped over an automated cleaner polishing the floor. He fled away from the world, toward imagined safety, in a sequestered part of Hardy Tower to which it seemed only he had access. Or perhaps the building only executed naked alcoholics; Tony could not be certain of the building\'92s ethics or morality.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Behind a stand of tropical palms arranged around a water feature, Tony encountered an elevator lobby with two pairs of sparkling jewel-encrusted doors. Worse than their gaudiness was their resolute state of shut-ness, and an apparent lack of a calling console. Perhaps Tony was meant to simply walk through the elevator doors too\'97he was quickly becoming accustomed to his new superpower. But what if there was no elevator car awaiting, and he fell down the elevator shaft? Perhaps he would not go splat at the bottom, but would pass through there also, all the way to the Cloudborne ground and travel on until he was cooked by the heat of the inner earth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>None of those happened because, before too much time had passed, one pair of doors opened, admitted Tony and, with the silent ease and grace of expensive engineering, catapulted him to a three-storey penthouse atop the tower.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Greetings, sir. My name is Fisher. I am delighted to finally meet you. We have been anticipating your arrival.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Have you?\'92 Tony asked the young King Creole Series ELVIS standing at ease in the egress from the elevator lobby, his feet wide apart and his hands clasped behind his back. Tony suspected the Elvi might be blocking his passage, which seemed at odds with his attire: an impeccably tailored velvet coat that accentuated the shoulders and narrowed the waist; a gold, angular brooch pinned to a lapel; a tastefully lustrous buttoned-up purple vest; a crisp high-collared white shirt; an aquamarine cravat; white gloves; breaches; and polished, buckled shoes. Warily, Tony stole a couple more paces away from the elevator, and heard its door close behind. \'91Have we met?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No, sir.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Then why are you happy to meet me?\'92 Tony could count on one hand with a thumb and a pinky amputated, the number of people who might be genuinely happy to see him. With sadness, he recalled another finger had been amputated: Halo. He may have been a psychopathic, misogynistic, serial killer who tried to off Sarah, but Halo was still Tony\'92s friend. Or had been. \'91What am I to you?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You are the one and lawful heir to Jamison Hardy,\'92 Fisher answered formally, his chin rising a fraction.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony\'92s mind seized up in a fashion only hard liquor could re-lubricate. \'91I\'92m the what now?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You are the\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Jamison Hardy? The once richest person in the world \'85 that Jamison Hardy?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, sir. I am sure you noticed your penthouse sits atop this, his eponymous tower.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, is suppose\'97wait \'85 
\f2\i my
\f1\i0  penthouse?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, sir.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony put his bloody hand to his forehead and worried some wrinkles into it as he tried figuring out what the actual tax was going on. \'91You don\'92t know me \'85 how\'97how do you know I\'92m Hardy\'92s heir?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You opened the Rose Door, sir.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The Rose Door? You mean the disappearing wall? I didn\'92t do anything.\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91More specifically, your DNA opened the door.\'92 When Tony looked at him blank, Fisher added, \'91The Rose Door sniffed your DNA as soon as you rubbed your nose against it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I see.\'92 Actually Tony didn\'92t see; his mind was foggier than the Layer after New Year\'92s Eve fireworks\'97which, of course, the Cloudborne never saw. \'91But, Danny \'85 may I call you Danny?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The King Creole Series\'92 ELVIS shifted uncomfortably on his feet. \'91I would prefer Fisher, sir.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How can you be sure, Fisher? I\'92m the only descendant? There could be thousands of us.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Mr Hardy enjoyed a good competition \'85 survival of the fittest and all that.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony never considered he might be the fittest at anything.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91In any case,\'92 Fisher continued. \'91You\'92re the first to have opened the Rose Door. You win.\'92\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Winner, winner, roadkill dinner
\f1\i0 , Tony thought, then visualised the unfortunate old man, sliced in half, almost  kosher style, by the Rose Door. How was he supposed to know it would shut so quickly after he\'92d passed through? \'91I\'92m afraid, there\'92s quite a mess down there,\'92 Tony said bitterly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, sir, already being cleaned up. A simple matter. It was only a cheap whisky, sir.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony\'92s shock ate through his muscles like acid. \'91Someone died down there.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, sir. A homeless Cloudborne,\'92 Fisher answered with professional detachment.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91He was my friend,\'92 Tony countered trenchantly, the volume of his own voice surprising him. Since when was an Elvi indifferent to a fellow Cloudborne?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91A friend?\'92 Fisher took a moment to consider. \'91Do you often befriend naked alcoholics?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I wouldn\'92t say often, exactly \'85\'92\
